+## MENUBAR-SHOWS-DEAD-LANES-AND-HASH-NAMES-01
+MENUBAR-SHOWS-DEAD-LANES-AND-HASH-NAMES-01 (P1, founder screenshot 2026-08-04 12:28): the SwiftBar status surface showed 'active 3' when only 1 lane was real. The two dead lanes (0db1da80, c33073fd) had dead worker PIDs AND correct terminal rows in dispatch-ledger.jsonl; leadv2-status-surface.sh:2850 fails to match them because the ledger keys on task_sig plus the HUMAN task_id while the live-process branch carries a different key, and even a match would only mark them 'closing' (:2852), never drop them. Second defect, same surface: lanes are labelled with the 8-char mission-hash while the human task name is in the same ledger row and the phase is in the lane journal — show '<task_id> - <phase> - <arm> <age>' and fall back to sig8 only when the name is unknown. Plugin repo.
+
+## SD-STATUSLINE-BURN-FIRSTCLASS-01
+SD-STATUSLINE-BURN-FIRSTCLASS-01: Give founder's statusline burn segment first-class width budget
+
+## STATUSLINE-FLICKER-PARTIAL-CACHE-01
+STATUSLINE-FLICKER-PARTIAL-CACHE-01 (P1, founder-observed 2026-07-30: 'постоянно вижу что статуслайн скачет из статуса в статус, то часть убралась, то добавилась'): the status line never computes synchronously. Per its own header (plugins/leadv2/scripts/leadv2-lane-status-line.sh ~38-52), the main path does exactly one of two instant things — cat a cached last-known-good line (~114-115), or, with no cache, print a minimal static line built from bash-builtin regex only — and then fires ONE detached, lockfile-throttled background refresher (throttle LEADV2_STATUSLINE_REFRESH_MIN_INTERVAL_S) that runs the real work (two jq reads, the wrapped user command, git-branch fallback, lane calc) and rewrites the cache FOR THE NEXT RENDER. Consequences the founder is actually seeing: (1) every line shown is the PREVIOUS refresh's result, so the line is always at least one refresh interval stale; (2) when one component of a refresh fails or times out — most likely the wrapped user command under its timeout 2 — that segment is missing from the line that gets cached, so it vanishes from the display and reappears on a later successful refresh; (3) a wholly failed refresh leaves the old cache in place, so values freeze and then jump. The visible symptom is segments appearing and disappearing between renders, which reads as an unreliable display even when the underlying data is fine. Required: a refresh must be all-or-nothing — never cache a partially-built line; if a component fails, keep the previous value for THAT component rather than dropping the segment, and never let a partial result overwrite a complete cached one. The line must be stable between renders: no segment may appear or disappear while the underlying lane set is unchanged. Distinct from STATUSLINE-SHOWS-LANES-QUESTIONMARK-01 (that one is the count and the fallback); this one is cache/refresh integrity. Verify by rendering repeatedly over several minutes with a fixed lane set and diffing consecutive lines — they must differ only where reality differed.
+
+## STATUSLINE-SHOWS-LANES-QUESTIONMARK-01
+STATUSLINE-SHOWS-LANES-QUESTIONMARK-01 (P1, founder-visible): the lane status line renders 'lanes ?' while four lanes are demonstrably alive. Evidence 2026-07-30T18:00: docs/leadv2/active.yaml is HEALTHY — it parses as {meta, sessions} with sessions carrying all four task_ids (0ef19f6a2491, 51ac3a8af960, fd7a0e1e00ed, 3e87a16f0f8a) — and the OS agrees (4 live claude -p workers, 5 subsessions, 2 launchers). So the data is fine and the reader is not. Two independent defects: (1) LATENCY FALLBACK — leadv2-lane-status-line.sh has a hard cheap-path budget and prints the static '<model> in <cwd> | lanes ?' (line ~141, and the cached-line path ~115) whenever the cold path blows it; its own header comment documents the cold path paying for six forks (2 jq reads, the wrapped user command, git branch fallback, the state-path resolver, python3+PyYAML), which is exactly what is happening here, so the founder sees '?' rather than a number. (2) WRONG PATH SHAPE — line ~60 derives liveness from docs/handoff/<task_id>/{session.log,fanout.log}, but lanes launched through the new detached launcher write docs/handoff/fanout-lane-<task_id>/launcher.log, so even on the fast path these lanes are invisible. This is the FIFTH handoff path shape the liveness readers must cover (see LANE-LIVENESS-BLIND-TO-FUNNEL-PATH-01: docs/handoff/<task-id>/, docs/handoff/dispatch-<hash>/, docs/leadv2/tasks/<id>/, and now docs/handoff/fanout-lane-<id>/) — fix the path set in ONE place, leadv2-lane-liveness.sh, and have the status line read only that. Founder impact: he has had no reliable read of whether work is running for most of 2026-07-30 and said so twice.
